WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.090
Hi.

00:00:01.090 --> 00:00:02.640
My name is Moin Nadeem,
and I'm currently

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:04.598
a junior in electrical
engineering and computer

00:00:04.598 --> 00:00:05.310
science.

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:08.910
My work broadly goes over
automated fake news detection,

00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:11.700
more, specifically automated
factory information.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:14.340
Given some claim
like, did Barack Obama

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:16.450
win the 2016
election, I provided

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:19.500
probability of how much this
claim is true or how much of it

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:21.060
may be false.

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:22.920
If you think about
how you may do this,

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:24.840
you would think, OK,
given some claim,

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:28.030
which evidences do I have to
support or refute this claim?

00:00:28.030 --> 00:00:30.900
So I've created a search engine
that given some query retrieves

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:32.910
all possible evidences
and retrieves how

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:36.460
much these evidences agree
or disagree with this claim.

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:38.670
Now, once you have
a list of evidences

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:40.420
that support or
refute this claim

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:42.420
and you know how much
they support or refute it,

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:46.230
you may want to also figure out
how credible is each speaker

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:48.510
because, for example,
one adversary may

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:51.543
want to flood the internet with
a few dozen small websites.

00:00:51.543 --> 00:00:53.460
And that doesn't mean
that this is as credible

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:55.470
as say, for example, an
established journalism

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:57.210
like the New York Times.

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:59.295
Because of this, we finally
take these evidences,

00:00:59.295 --> 00:01:01.170
and we know how much
they agree and disagree.

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:03.540
And we weight these
by who's speaking

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:05.400
and how much credibility
do they have.

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.680
Finally, we can return
this weighted sum

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.270
over who's speaking, how
much credibility they have,

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:12.750
and what are they saying.

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:15.700
More specifically, I'm
working on the first pipeline,

00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:17.010
which is evidence selection.

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:19.800
Given some query, I return
a list of sorted documents

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:22.890
representing how
relevant each claim is

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:26.547
and what number the document
is in that position.

00:01:26.547 --> 00:01:28.380
One way to think about
this is I'm basically

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:29.370
building Google search.

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:32.760
You type in some query, and you
get a list of sorted documents.

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:36.090
Google Search has a 56%
chance of showing you

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:39.180
what you want to see as a
first result on the list.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:40.610
For the search
engine I've built,

00:01:40.610 --> 00:01:43.020
I have a 79% chance
of showing you

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:45.673
the result you want to see as
a first result on the list.

00:01:45.673 --> 00:01:47.340
This means that we
have more information

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:50.370
to [INAUDIBLE] more accurate
fake news assessment.

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:52.590
Check out our demo
on factor.app,

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:54.120
and I'm happy to
discuss this more.

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:55.880
Thank you.

